                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_problem_right: `<strong>That's the real problem.</strong> You looked past the written brief and named the stalling transformation, the disengaged middle managers, and the safety signal. That is diagnosis before design — and it is exactly what lets you out-design a generic R$5.8M brand and a R$2.5M commodity.`,
                    coach_r1_leadership:    `<strong>You've softened the brief, not reframed it.</strong> 'Generic leadership skills' is closer to what the RFP literally asked for than to what the discovery call revealed. The real issue is a transformation stalling on weak frontline leadership and disengagement. Design against the cause, not the symptom.`,
                    coach_r1_literal:       `<strong>You took the brief at face value.</strong> A polished generic leadership curriculum is exactly what the RFP asked for — and exactly what the global school sells for R$5.8M and the local rival for R$2.5M. If you're also selling generic, why would Aratu pay your price? The diagnosis is the differentiator.`,
                    coach_r1_safety:        `<strong>Safety is part of the signal, not the whole diagnosis.</strong> The two incidents are a symptom of weak frontline leadership inside a stalling transformation. Mandatory safety training treats the symptom; the leadership and engagement layer is where the cause lives.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the real business problem AND the metrics Aratu's board will judge — transformation milestones, engagement, safety. That is the CHRO's language, not the school's learning metrics. Now every design choice has a north star.`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction is the school's metric, not the board's.</strong> The CHRO has a board offsite and two safety incidents to answer for — happy sheets won't help her there. Diagnose against the business results her board will actually judge.`,
                    coach_r1_noreframe:     `<strong>You took the request without testing it.</strong> In six months, when engagement hasn't moved and there's another incident, whose program gets blamed? Reframing feels risky now — but delivering the wrong program on time is the bigger risk. Challenge the brief without sounding like you're refusing the work.`,
                    coach_r2_anchor_right:  `<strong>The journey has a spine.</strong> Anchoring on frontline leadership that drives engagement and safe operations through the transformation ties every later choice back to the diagnosis. Now the architecture has something to be justified against.`,
                    coach_r2_theory:        `<strong>Frameworks are not the outcome.</strong> Anchoring on classic leadership theory drifts back toward the generic program. The board won't judge whether managers can name a model — it will judge whether behaviour and the transformation actually moved.`,
                    coach_r2_generic:       `<strong>A generic competency set is a course, not a journey.</strong> 'Becoming a better leader' could be sold to any client — which is precisely why it loses to a R$2.5M commodity. Anchor on the Aratu-specific outcome the diagnosis revealed.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's a 70-20-10 journey.</strong> Action-learning on real Aratu problems and peer coaching carry the transfer, immersive in-person builds the trust and depth, and digital reinforces and scales. Behaviour changes back at work — not just in the classroom.`,
                    coach_r2_lecture:       `<strong>This is an event, not a journey.</strong> With so little action-learning and peer work, you're betting behaviour will change from instruction alone. It won't. The transfer happens on real Aratu problems and between peers — that's where the weight belongs.`,
                    coach_r2_alldigital:    `<strong>Digital scales reinforcement, it doesn't build trust.</strong> A near-all-digital journey is cheap and convenient but won't shift disengaged middle managers through a painful transformation. Trust and depth need immersive in-person and peer work too.`,
                    coach_r2_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction-only measurement measures the wrong thing.</strong> Level-1 happy sheets tell you if people enjoyed it, not whether their behaviour or the business moved. Build in Level 3 and Level 4, baselined before launch, or you can't prove impact to the board.`,
                    coach_r2_nomeasure:     `<strong>No measurement is no proof.</strong> If you can't show behaviour change and business results, you can't defend your price against a R$2.5M bid — and you can't tell the board the transformation moved. Design measurement in from the start.`,
                    coach_r3_facblend:      `<strong>Smart faculty staffing.</strong> Best-fit leads on the high-stakes transformation and safety modules, strong mid-tier faculty elsewhere — you protect impact where it matters and protect margin where it doesn't.`,
                    coach_r3_allsenior:     `<strong>All-senior faculty is gold-plating.</strong> Premium retainers on every module is wonderful and expensive — it's the fastest way to break the margin floor. Reserve the best-fit leads for the modules that actually carry the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r3_alljunior:     `<strong>All-junior faculty hollows out the design.</strong> You protected margin by removing the credibility and depth the transformation modules need. The diagnosis is the differentiator — under-staffing it is how you lose to the commodity.`,
                    coach_r3_offshelf:      `<strong>Off-the-shelf content is no longer custom.</strong> You've saved on development by buying exactly what the global school sells. Reuse the generic layers, yes — but the Aratu-specific core has to be bespoke or the diagnosis evaporates.`,
                    coach_r3_smart:         `<strong>This is design and pricing as one decision.</strong> Reusing proven content for the generic layers and building bespoke only for the Aratu core protects both the diagnosis and the 30% floor. That's how you defend full value without breaking margin.`,
                    coach_r3_matchlow:      `<strong>You dropped to beat the local rival on price.</strong> You've thrown away your margin AND signalled to procurement that your price was inflated all along — which puts the whole R$11M account on the table at the next renewal. You don't win a commodity fight by becoming a commodity.`,
                    coach_r3_thin:          `<strong>You thinned the design to lift margin.</strong> The number looks safe, but you've quietly gutted the journey that carries the diagnosis. A cheap, generic proposal under the ceiling is exactly what the R$2.5M rival is already selling.`,
                    coach_r3_marginbreak:   `<strong>This breaks the floor.</strong> A wonderful journey at this cost lands below 30% contribution margin — the unit won't staff it. Design without a commercial model is a wish, not a proposal. What comes out, and how do you protect the diagnosis while you cut?`,
                    coach_r4_restructure:   `<strong>That's how you defend value under pressure.</strong> Holding the price while offering a scope-linked restructure tells procurement the price reflects real scope, not slack — and protects both the diagnosis and the margin instead of capitulating.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding flat is defensible — if you can carry the value story.</strong> A flat 'no' protects margin but risks the relationship unless the outcome case is strong. Pair the hold with the business-impact argument the CHRO can take to procurement.`,
                    coach_r4_concede:       `<strong>You conceded the full 12%.</strong> You kept procurement happy by handing back margin and signalling your price was soft. The discount is easy to give and impossible to take back — and the account just learned your number is negotiable on demand.`,
                    coach_r4_scale_smart:   `<strong>You scaled honestly.</strong> Re-engineering cohorts and leaning on digital and peer layers absorbs the +40 without gutting the per-participant experience — and you repriced the unit economics instead of pretending scale is free.`,
                    coach_r4_absorb:        `<strong>You added 40 people and changed nothing.</strong> Either your original price had enormous slack — so why did you quote it? — or you've quietly gutted the per-participant experience. Adding 22% scale is not free; say honestly what it does to cohort design and unit cost.`,
                    coach_r4_dilute:        `<strong>Quietly thinning the experience is the worst of both.</strong> You kept the price and degraded the learning the diagnosis depends on. When engagement doesn't move, this is the design that gets blamed.`,
                    coach_r4_swap:          `<strong>Swapping the lead out drops the credibility.</strong> Whoever is free is not the best-fit transformation faculty the diagnosis needs. Protect the diagnosis-critical sessions with the lead, even at reduced availability.`,
                    coach_r4_cram:          `<strong>Cramming the lead into 50% is fragile.</strong> Hope is not a coverage plan — if the lead slips, the diagnosis-critical modules collapse. Pair them with a co-deliver/understudy so the critical sessions are protected.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>That's an outcome-framed pitch.</strong> The CHRO hears what changes in her managers' behaviour and her business metrics — the transformation, the engagement, the safety. That's what lands in a board offsite, not a tour of modalities and faculty CVs.`,
                    coach_r4_features:      `<strong>You pitched features to a CHRO who needs outcomes.</strong> Four minutes of modalities, faculty CVs and platform screens won't help her at a board offsite with two safety incidents to answer for. Sell the behaviour change and the business metrics, not the architecture.`,
                    coach_r4_pricepitch:    `<strong>Leading on price concedes the commodity frame.</strong> If your pitch is mostly about how competitive your number is, you've agreed to be compared on price — exactly the fight the R$2.5M rival wins. Lead with the outcome only you can deliver.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
